        "max": 1.0264,
        "mean": 1.0155
      }
    },
    "THE_UNRESOLVED_POINT": "the spec names the target value (1.002) but NOT the width it was observed at. In this corpus 1.002 lands at k=14 (mean 1.0026); at k=12 this corpus gives 1.0155. Both readings are live because the THEORETICAL random-occupancy value at k=12 for 82,800 draws over 4^12 cells is also 1.0025 -- so a corpus with near-random 12-mer occupancy would show 1.002 at k=12, while this one shows 1.0155 there because real DNA repeats and collides more. P2 should confirm which width its 1.002 was measured at.",
    "WHY_THE_HUMAN_RESULT_SURVIVES_THE_AMBIGUITY": "the ~10 threshold is absolute, derived in the spec from the next-base distribution having 4 outcomes (~10 observations per context is ~2.5 per outcome). It is NOT derived from E. coli. The human ratios at k=12 are 1.840 (K562) and 1.547 (HepG2), far below 10 on any reading, so the calibration validates the CODE and the verdict rests on the absolute bar."
  },
  "RESULT_the_crossing_point_is_the_deliverable": {
    "K562": {
      "first_width_below_10": 11,
      "crossing_bracket_bp": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "k10": 11.4434,
      "k11": 3.7908,
      "k12": 1.8403
    },
    "HepG2": {
      "first_width_below_10": 10,
      "crossing_bracket_bp": [
        8,
        10
      ],
      "k10": 7.6489,
      "k11": 2.8148,
      "k12": 1.547
    },
    "THE_AMBIGUITY_THE_SPEC_ASKED_FOR_RATHER_THAN_A_ROUNDED_SIDE": "the two cells DISAGREE at exactly k=10: K562 reads 11.44, marginally ABOVE the bar of 10, while HepG2 reads 7.65, below it. So 10 bp is usable in one cell and not the other, and this is precisely the case the bar's never-pool rule anticipates. Reported as a disagreement rather than averaged into a single crossing.",
    "at_12bp_neither_cell_is_close_to_usable": "K562 1.840 and HepG2 1.547 against a bar of 10. Not near the decision boundary, so this part is NOT ambiguous."
  },
  "AN_INTERNAL_CONSISTENCY_CHECK_THAT_PASSED": {
    "the_check": "if the measurement is behaving, a larger corpus should buy extra reach at exactly log4(size ratio), because each additional bp multiplies the context space by 4",
    "corpus_size_ratio_at_k12_human_over_ecoli600": 134.0,
    "predicted_extra_reach_bp": 3.53,
    "observed_shift_bp": "E. coli crosses between 6 and 8 bp; K562 between 10 and 11 -- about 3 to 4 bp",
    "reading": "the shift matches the alphabet scaling, so the human corpus shows no anomalous density in either direction. A mismatch here would have meant the statistic was measuring something other than occupancy."
  },
